The precedent for the same defect in the same trait is climb batch 9's rung S (1e176516c). It wrote `abstract get(i:ZZ32): Char` and `abstract asDebugStringIndented(indent: ZZ32): String` on String's api and gave each of the four string objects a body (explorations/compile-ladder/rung-string-slips/REPORT.md, section 4, 'Names the api lacked').

The team's own api already wrote `abstract splitWithOffsets()` and `abstract split()` for members the component's trait never declares (Library/FortressLibrary.fsi:2498-2499; also present at a874948ac). The rule is the skill's library.md, 'Designing a change': write abstract in the api on each member the component leaves without a body. This is P-27's practice, and F-282 confirms the export check reads it.

Other sites in FortressLibrary.fsi: none left. The file has 165 abstract lines at the base (88 at a874948ac) and 174 now. Of the export error's 15 entries, String's was the only one of this kind (CLIMB-BATCH-22.md:189-203), and the per-site list after the edit shows no other.

The bodies follow existing library shapes:
- the two balanced bodies follow SubString's balanced, which answers self when balanced (Library/String.fss:373-376);
- EmptyString's isBalanced follows FlatString's isBalanced = true (Library/FlatString.fss:48);
- CatString's range split follows its own uncheckedSubstring (Library/String.fss:148-161);
- the guard follows SubString IN's '(¬ r.isEmpty) AND: str.rangeContains(...)' (Library/String.fss:431).

I followed rung-T.patch, which applies these precedents, unchanged.
